>       Capabilities: enum
>       Items: 'PCM Out' 'H/W In 0' 'H/W In 1' 'IEC958 In L' 'IEC958 In R'
>       Item0: 'H/W In 0'
>     Simple mixer control 'H/W',3
>       Capabilities: enum
>       Items: 'PCM Out' 'H/W In 0' 'H/W In 1' 'IEC958 In L' 'IEC958 In R'
>       Item0: 'H/W In 1'
>     Simple mixer control 'H/W',4
>       Capabilities: enum
>       Items: 'PCM Out' 'H/W In 0' 'H/W In 1' 'IEC958 In L' 'IEC958 In R'
>       Item0: 'IEC958 In L'
>     Simple mixer control 'H/W',5
>       Capabilities: enum
>       Items: 'PCM Out' 'H/W In 0' 'H/W In 1' 'IEC958 In L' 'IEC958 In R'
>       Item0: 'IEC958 In R'
> 
> These seem to control the digital loop-back feature, which is
> described in section 4.2.4 of the Envy24HT data sheet.  The first two
> "H/W" seem to correspond to the left and right stereo channel.  I
> guess that the others correspond to center, LFE, and rear.

They are routing tables on vt1724.  The real names of these controls
are "H/W Playback Route", and "Playback Route" is omitted by the mixer
abstraction layer.

Each channel is indicated with index numbers because the output analog
channels might be non-bundled, just independent mono outputs.  Also
the driver keeps generic names there.  That's why 'H/W' is used.

> Also I find it a but suspicious that there is only one "PCM Out" value
> and not "PCM Out L" and "PCM Out R".

If you choose PCM as a playback source, you have no option to choose
left or right.  The certain channel is always bound to the certain
output.  OTOH, you can choose either left or right channel of SPDIF
input.

So, this appearance is logically correct, but may be puzzling for
boards with stere-jacks (like revo).


